OER = Oxygen Evolution Reaction: 2H₂O → O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ (or 4OH⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ in base). It's the anode (oxidation) half-reaction of water
  electrolysis — the other half is HER (hydrogen evolution) at the cathode, and together they split water into H₂ and O₂.

  Why it matters practically: Electrolyzers are the main way to make "green" hydrogen — run electricity (ideally renewable) through water
  instead of steam-reforming natural gas, and you get H₂ fuel with no carbon in the process. Hydrogen made this way is a candidate for
  decarbonizing things electrification struggles with: steel/cement production, long-haul shipping and aviation, seasonal/grid-scale energy
  storage, and as a feedstock for green ammonia. So OER catalyst performance is directly a bottleneck on how cheap and efficient that whole
  "hydrogen economy" pathway is.

  Why OER specifically is the hard part. HER (the H₂-making side) is comparatively easy — it's a 2-electron process and Pt is already an
  excellent, well-understood catalyst for it. OER is a 4-electron, 4-proton process with multiple bound-oxygen intermediates (*OH → *O → *OOH →
  O₂) that each need roughly comparable amounts of energy to form. Because they can't all be simultaneously easy on the same catalyst surface,
  there's an intrinsic energy penalty called the scaling relation — it's the reason OER is kinetically sluggish on essentially every known
  catalyst and needs a large overpotential (extra voltage beyond the thermodynamic 1.23 V) to run at a useful rate. OER overpotential is the
  single biggest efficiency loss in most electrolyzers, which is why so much catalysis research (including this project) focuses there rather
  than on HER.

  Why it's a materials problem, not just a chemistry one. The best-known OER catalysts are IrO₂ and RuO₂ — both scarce, expensive precious
  metals (Ir is one of the rarest elements in Earth's crust), which is a real bottleneck if OER-based electrolysis is going to scale to
  gigawatt/industrial levels. That scarcity is the actual motivation for this project's HEA (high-entropy alloy) approach: mix Ir/Ru with
  cheaper, abundant 3d transition metals (Cr, Co, etc.) in a disordered multi-metal oxide, and try to find compositions that keep OER activity
  high (measured via the ΔG/η descriptor from the last question) while diluting out most of the precious metal.

  Why it connects to your project's actual finding. The theoretical descriptor used to screen candidates (CHE-derived η, computed from DFT) is
  only as trustworthy as the DFT itself — and your project's central result is that standard screening pipelines can silently get that
  descriptor wrong (Hubbard-U sensitivity, the symmetry trap, magnetic multistability) in ways that look like a real discovery but aren't.
  That's the throughline: OER is important because it's the efficiency bottleneck in green hydrogen production, precious-metal scarcity makes
  finding better catalysts economically urgent, and your project's contribution is showing where the computational search for those catalysts
  can quietly mislead itself.
